# Repo-root ignores. Each package also carries its own .gitignore so it stays
# self-contained if the two are ever split into separate repos — that overlap is
# deliberate and costs nothing.

# --- build output (never commit; PyPI/npm build these from source) ---
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/
*.tsbuildinfo

# --- dependencies ---
node_modules/
.venv/
venv/

# --- test / tool caches ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.tox/
coverage/
.coverage
*.tgz

# --- editors / OS ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# --- ⚠ founder-only, never published ---------------------------------------
# MAINTAINERS.md is an internal ops document, not customer documentation: it
# names production infrastructure, hosting providers, deploy prerequisites and
# the private repo layout. None of that is a credential, but it is fleet
# topology — the same class of fact this product deliberately strips from its own
# API responses (`Machine.HostWireGuardIP` is `json:"-"` precisely so tenants
# cannot learn it). Publishing it in the SDK repo would hand it over anyway.
#
# ⚠ And keep this comment vague. The first draft of it named the exact address
# it was written to protect, which would have published the thing the ignore
# rule exists to withhold.
#
# It stays HERE, beside the code it describes, so it does not rot in a folder
# nobody opens. Outside contributors get CONTRIBUTING.md instead.
#
# ⚠ If you ever need it in a repo, put it in the PRIVATE one — never this one,
# and never via `git add -f`.
MAINTAINERS.md

# --- ⚠ never commit credentials -------------------------------------------
# This repo is PUBLIC. An SDK is the one place a real API key is most likely to
# be pasted "just to test", and a leaked sk_live_ key can create machines and
# spend a wallet. Keys belong in the environment (SISTEMO_API_KEY), never a file.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
.npmrc
.pypirc
